WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:00.570
Good morning, John

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.780
I just sent a Twitter survey out to my followers asking them some of their thoughts on starting a business

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:08.060
15% of those over 18 said that they'd already started the business

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.660
55% said they had considered starting one.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:14.520
Only 11% said that they weren't at all interested in starting their own business

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:18.020
But while I think there's a strong entrepreneurial attitude among young people right now

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:19.860
the rate at which we in America are starting small businesses,

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:22.380
and young people in particular, is dropping...

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:23.260
fast.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.360
And the easy thing to do is to put this on young people themselves.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:30.780
We aren't interested, or we crave the stability of a normal job, or we fear failure.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:36.320
And indeed the social anxiety, uncertainty, and fear of failure all showed up high up in my survey.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:39.040
And starting a business isn't for the faint of heart.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.460
You have to take risks you have to jump into stuff that you don't understand or know very well.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:45.560
You have to do stuff you don't want to do. You have to hire people, and you have to fire people.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.520
A lot of the time it sucks to own a business

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.540
I'd say this is a person who owns businesses, like, there's parts that are not super fun.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:56.360
And I think people know that, but this very clearly isn't just about people making different choices.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.760
As schools have fought to attract students, the cost of education has skyrocketed

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.500
And thus so has the burden of student debt and that debt pushes people toward making safer decisions

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.800
And maybe toward fearing failure more.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:14.800
In exactly the same way the skyrocketing costs of health care in America have made people make safer decisions.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:19.440
When asked what their number one reason for not starting a business was, about half of people said that

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:22.540
that was a personal choice and about half of people said it was something

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:26.040
structural and the biggest structural one of course is just access to capital.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.400
And the money things have gotten kind of weird lately.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.680
A lot more money is coming from venture capitalists,

00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:32.200
Who are just like Super rich people.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.250
who basically

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.400
want their money to come back ten times

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.120
so they mostly invest in tech where there's a lot of opportunity for huge growth. Businesses that

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.520
probably have a safer path to a smaller market get ignored and banks are kind of

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:49.240
focusing less and less on the thing that they're supposed to be doing which is to help people create new value in the world.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.160
Community banks now make up just 13% of the banking sector

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:55.700
but they still make 43% of the small business loans.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:00.100
As starting a business gets more complicated people who have money might see it as

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:04.760
simpler and probably a better use of their time to just invest that money in the stock market.

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:09.000
Meaning in big gigantic entrenched companies rather than in their own small businesses.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:15.900
And that's especially true if you have a lot of money, and more money is in the hands of people who have a lot of it.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.720
If you have a ton of money starting up a small businesses like an

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.940
inconsequential investment in exchange for a whole lot of work.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:26.520
And then the massive amount of money pumped into the stock market pushes these

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:31.220
gigantic businesses to grow or die, and that results in these massive

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.700
behemoths taking over not just old markets

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:36.980
but new markets the way that Amazon and Netflix have done.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.200
Meanwhile in the middle-class wealth has actually decreased

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.100
so the people who really want to work their butts off to make a new thing happen

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:45.900
might not have access to the money to do that.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.060
The final reason I'm going to talk about why young people might not be starting

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:53.180
businesses is because I think they are. This is generation 1099, side-hustling contractors.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:58.800
People making it work with a combination of their podcast, their Etsy store, their post mates, and their day job.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.380
Every one of those side hustles is basically a sole proprietorship.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:06.860
If you count that way this generation is starting up businesses like never before. But the path to hiring that second person

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:13.679
when all competitive supply chains are global and when employment regulations and business taxes are mountains of red tape?

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.000
It's unclear if it's there at all,

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.549
and if it is it might just be uninteresting. This isn't a simple story people much smarter than me are doing much better

00:03:20.739 --> 00:03:22.560
research than I have done to look at this,

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:23.470
but I want more people

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:29.459
instead of fewer people to know the joy of starting your own thing, of giving somebody else a job, of putting a team together

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.858
to create value where there was no value before.

00:03:31.858 --> 00:03:33.479
If you've got thoughts on this or advice on this,

00:03:33.549 --> 00:03:38.249
or just want to tell us what you would do if you had access to the capital you need to do it,

00:03:38.249 --> 00:03:41.867
I'm really looking forward to the comments on this video. I'll be checking them out all weekend.

00:03:41.867 --> 00:03:42.960
John. I'll see you on Tuesday

